Operating System-Level "Tofu Code Display" Functional Requirements Specification

1. Background and Problem Statement

1.1 Scale of the Problem
According to incomplete statistics, over 60 million people in China have names that include
rare/obscure characters (i.e., characters beyond the 6,763 Chinese characters defined in
GB/T 2312‑1980; among these, approximately 10 million people have names containing characters
outside the basic set \u4e00-\u9fa5). In Shanghai alone, among its 25 million permanent
residents, about 40,000–50,000 citizens have names containing such rare characters.

These individuals consistently encounter obstacles in various scenarios, including real‑name
authentication, certificate applications, banking, medical registration, travel check‑in,
and other services.

Common difficulties faced by people with rare‑character names include:
    • Input methods cannot find the character;
    • The rare‑character name cannot match the account name exactly;
    • The character is treated as an illegal character or converted to "?" during data exchange;
    • Real‑name services cannot be processed due to data transmission issues;
    • The character cannot be transferred between different hardware devices;
    • Terminal device font libraries do not support the character.

1.2 Two Core Pain Points

Pain Point 1: PUA Encoding Used in ID Cards Is Not Publicly Disclosed, and Glyphs Are Not Displayed
To address the issue of rare characters in names, the Ministry of Public Security has established
a dedicated font library for population information. However, a large number of rare characters
in this font library use PUA (Private Use Area) codes.

PUA encoding causes serious problems:
    1. Encoding is not public: PUA is a private‑use area reserved in the Unicode standard, and
      each system can define its own mapping. However, the PUA mapping of the public security
      population information font library has not been disclosed to the public, making it
      impossible for third‑party systems to recognise the corresponding Chinese characters for these codes.
    2. One character, multiple codes: The same rare character may use a PUA code in the public
       security system and a formal code in the national standard GB 18030.
       Without normalisation, systems cannot match them, leading to direct service refusal.
    3. Cross‑system incompatibility: Although the public security system can display PUA characters,
       other industry systems (banking, social security, transportation, healthcare, etc.) 
       do not support these PUA codes, resulting in characters being displayed as "□", "?", or blank.

Pain Point 2: Newly Encoded Chinese Characters Are Not Available for Download, and Glyphs Are Not Displayed

As of August 1, 2023, the mandatory national standard GB 18030‑2022 (covering up to Extension F,
with a total of 87,887 Chinese characters) took effect.

As of October 1, 2025, Amendment No. 1 to GB 18030‑2022 took effect, adding Extensions G, H, and I,
totalling 9,793 new characters.

Extension J was released in 2025, containing 4,304 characters (of which only 4 are used in personal
names), but it has not yet been incorporated into GB 18030‑2022 or its Amendment No. 1.

In total, the standard requires government and public service products and systems to support
approximately 100,000 Chinese characters at Implementation Level 3.

However, the reality is:
    1. Insufficient OS font coverage: Currently, default fonts installed in Windows, macOS, 
       and mainstream Linux distributions cover only about 30,000 commonly used Chinese characters.
       Even if the operating system supports the Unicode encoding, if no glyph data exists for
       the character in the system fonts, it will still be displayed as a square or garbled.

    2. Users cannot install missing fonts themselves: Super‑large font libraries that comply
       with GB 18030‑2022 Level 3 are mostly commercially licensed fonts, and ordinary users
       cannot obtain and install them for free.

    3. Service character set is not publicly disclosed: Although the full character set
       (approximately 100,000 characters) can theoretically display all Chinese characters,
       the actual "service character set" used in daily public services covers only about
       30,000–50,000 characters. This character set has not been made public, and the
        government does not provide free fonts for citizens to download.
        This aggravates the issue described in point 2 above — users cannot even determine
        whether a missing character falls within the service character set, turning what
        could be an expected display problem into an uncertainty problem.

    4. Application‑level solutions are insufficient: Even if the browser or application
       implements a "tofu code" solution, numerous system‑level scenarios such as
       system dialogs, file managers, consoles, and third‑party applications still
       cannot display these newly encoded Chinese characters.

2. Functional Requirements

2.1 Core Feature: OS‑Level "Tofu Code Display"
At the operating system's font rendering engine level: when a character cannot be rendered 
because no glyph is available in the system fonts, instead of displaying a generic 
empty square (□) or question mark (?), the system shall automatically display the
character's Unicode code point in a visual format inside a square box, with the following format:

Character Type	    Code Point Range	Display Format	                     Example

BMP characters    	U+0000 – U+FFFF	  Top row: first 2 hex digits,         U+E863 
                                      bottom row: last 2 hex digits	       → top "E8" / bottom "63"

SIP/TIP characters	U+10000 and above	Top row: "U" + first 2 hex digits,   U+2C386 
                                      bottom row: last 3 hex digits	       → top "U2C" / bottom "386"

Display specifications:
    • Box size: exactly one standard Chinese character position (1em × 1em)
    • Box style: black border, light background, monospace font for the internal encoding
    • Alignment: vertically centred with adjacent characters

2.2 Priority by Scenario

Priority	Scenario	Description
P0       System‑level text rendering	File Explorer, desktop, taskbar, system dialogs, etc.
P0	     Built‑in system applications	Notepad, WordPad, console/terminal, etc.
P1	     Browser engine	Edge/Chrome rendering based on the system font engine
P1	     Third‑party GUI apps	Applications using system font APIs (Win32/WPF/Qt, etc.)
P2	     Printing subsystem	Print preview and actual print output

3. Suggested Technical Implementation Path
3.1 Font Rendering Engine Modifications

Windows platform:
    • Modify the fallback logic of DirectWrite and GDI font rendering engines.
    • When all fonts in the fallback chain lack a glyph for the target character,
      trigger the "Tofu Code Display" rendering path.
    • Leverage experiences from open‑source projects like MacType that intercept
      and enhance font rendering.

macOS platform:
    • Modify the font fallback mechanism in Core Text.
    • Use APIs such as CTFontCopyDefaultCascadeListForLanguages to detect missing glyphs.
    • Insert a "Tofu Code Display" renderer at the end of the system fallback chain.

Linux platform:
    • Modify the rendering pipeline in Fontconfig and FreeType / HarfBuzz.
    • Invoke the "Tofu Code Display" module when font matching fails.

3.2 System API Extensions
Add or extend system font APIs to provide the following capabilities:

cpp
// Conceptual API design
enum GlyphRenderMode {
    GlyphRenderMode_Normal,      // Normal rendering
    GlyphRenderMode_TofuCode,    // Tofu Code Display (show Unicode encoding)
    GlyphRenderMode_TofuBlank    // Traditional blank square
};

// System‑wide setting
SystemParametersInfo(SPI_SETGLYPHMODE, GlyphRenderMode_TofuCode, ...);

// Per‑process override (allows applications to customise)
SetProcessGlyphRenderMode(GlyphRenderMode_TofuCode);

3.3 System Settings Entry
Add a new option under the "Fonts & Display" or "Accessibility" section in the operating system settings:
    • Option name: "Display mode for missing glyphs"
    • Options:
        ◦ Traditional blank square (□) (default, for compatibility)
        ◦ Show Unicode encoding (Tofu Code Display)
    • Description: "When the system cannot display a character due to missing font glyphs,
      show its Unicode code point instead."

4. Expected Benefits

4.1 Directly Benefited Population
    • 60 million residents with rare‑character names: they can "see" the encoding of their own names
      in any scenario, rather than blanks or question marks.
    • All public service personnel who need to process rare‑character information: they can visually
      identify the character encoding, facilitating manual verification and handling.

4.2 Social Benefits

    1. Reduces the burden of "proving I am myself": People with rare‑character names often need to
       repeatedly prove their identity because systems cannot display their names correctly. 
       "Tofu Code Display" makes the encoding visible, enabling easier manual comparison and communication.

    2. Promotes encoding standardisation: When users can see the Unicode code point of a character,
       it creates pressure for various industries to gradually unify to national standard encodings,
       accelerating the migration from PUA to formal codes.
    3. Fills the information gap caused by missing fonts: Even if the operating system does not
       have a super‑large font installed, users can still know that "there is a character here
       with this specific code" rather than facing a complete information black hole.
    4. Reduces public service costs: Decreases the need for offline processing, manual verification,
       complaint handling, and other steps caused by unreadable rare characters.

4.3 Commercial Benefits

    • Serves as a differentiating feature for operating systems, demonstrating deep understanding
      and care for Chinese users and public service scenarios.
    • Aligns with the spirit of the mandatory national standard GB 18030‑2022 and its Amendment No. 1.
    • Enables a value‑added service: offer users a "one‑click install super‑large font" option
      (after the "Tofu Code Display" is in place, users may choose to install fonts for normal rendering).

5. Promotion Recommendations

5.1 Policy Foundations

    1.GB 18030‑2022 "Information Technology – Chinese Coded Character Set": A mandatory national standard 
      requiring government and public service products and systems to support approximately 100,000 
      Chinese characters (including the additions from Amendment No. 1).

    2.JR/T 0253‑2022 "Financial Services – Guidelines for Handling Rare Characters": An industry standard 
      for the financial sector, providing guidelines for rare character processing.

    3."Opinions on Strengthening Digital Chinese Construction and Promoting the Information Technology 
      Development of Language and Writing": Jointly issued by the Ministry of Education, the National 
      Language Commission, and the Cyberspace Administration of China, promoting the informatisation 
      of language and writing.

