5.3 Reference Examples
    • Some OS or application has already supported in certain scenarios, which can display
      missing glyphs as squares, or display squares with unicode(ie FireFox)
    • Some software venders "Rare Character Solution" already addresses rare character 
      display and input issues on Windows and Linux systems.
    • Some font vendors already support GB18030-2022 full set glyphs to extension F, but
      not include its Amendment No. 1（unicode extension GHI).

6. Conclusion

"Tofu Code Display" is not a permanent solution to the problem of displaying rare characters 
at the OS level — the ultimate solution requires:
    • Government authorities to unify standards and, drawing on the experience of Japan, to provide 
      free fonts covering the modern service character set (approximately 50,000 characters) to citizens;
    • Simultaneously, font vendors to provide commercial or open‑source fonts covering the full character
      set of approximately 100,000 characters;
    • All industry systems to fully adapt to GB 18030‑2022 (including Amendment No. 1);
    • A complete migration from PUA to formal Unicode codes.

"Tofu Code Display" is a temporary bridge that ensures the 10 million people with rare‑character names and the 50 million 
service professionals no longer face blanks and question marks before that ultimate goal is achieved.

It makes every character's encoding visible, readable, and communicable — when the system cannot tell you "what character 
this is," it at least tells you "which code this is." Before the ultimate vision of "unified script, unified code" is 
realised, this is a low‑cost, high‑benefit feature that can immediately improve the digital life experience of 
tens of millions of people.

This requirement document is freely available for distribution and submission to operating system vendors. 
We encourage its wide dissemination to drive industry‑wide adoption.
